WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.070 --> 00:00:03.399
Film Courage: We had a question come in from
Jacob.

00:00:03.399 --> 00:00:07.990
How do you share your ideas without getting
run over by the other writers or bullying

00:00:07.990 --> 00:00:08.990
them?

00:00:08.990 --> 00:00:13.090
Erik Bork, Author/Screenwriter: Without getting
run over by them or bullying them?

00:00:13.090 --> 00:00:14.099
How do you share your ideas?

00:00:14.099 --> 00:00:19.199
Film Courage: Yes…I’m assuming that it
would mean people kind of poking holes in

00:00:19.199 --> 00:00:23.810
your idea and allowing them (maybe) to sway
you from your voice?

00:00:23.810 --> 00:00:25.000
And I’m just assuming (sorry Jacob).

00:00:25.000 --> 00:00:29.380
Erik: That’s the tricky part, you can’t
avoid…I mean pick who you share it with

00:00:29.380 --> 00:00:34.210
wisely and pick the time that you share it
and the reason why you are sharing it and

00:00:34.210 --> 00:00:39.489
what you’re looking for from sharing it
and what you tell them when you share it but

00:00:39.489 --> 00:00:43.969
even if you do all of those things, people
are out of our control and they may come back

00:00:43.969 --> 00:00:51.899
to you with opinions and reactions that are
very unhelpful to you or emotionally discouraging

00:00:51.899 --> 00:00:54.220
or devastating to you and you may not be able
to avoid that.

00:00:54.220 --> 00:00:58.640
I mean you can certainly work on handling
your own reaction but you can’t control

00:00:58.640 --> 00:01:01.329
what they’re going to say as much as you
might try.

00:01:01.329 --> 00:01:05.199
But if you’re conscious about all of those
things and sort of being targeted about who

00:01:05.199 --> 00:01:10.200
you are sharing it with and why and all those
things and also noticing that they may say

00:01:10.200 --> 00:01:16.130
things that I don’t like and I don’t have
to let it destroy my opinion about whatever

00:01:16.130 --> 00:01:17.130
I am writing.

00:01:17.130 --> 00:01:21.860
However I’m open, I’m doing it because
I’m open to other opinions and I want to

00:01:21.860 --> 00:01:24.159
have a sense of how others are reacting to
it.

00:01:24.159 --> 00:01:26.110
Any one person is not enough though.

00:01:26.110 --> 00:01:31.070
It’s probably you need a consensus of people
and people who know something about what you’re

00:01:31.070 --> 00:01:34.750
trying to do and people who understand the
craft.

00:01:34.750 --> 00:01:40.350
Unfortunately other aspiring writers might
not be as valuable of opinion to you.

00:01:40.350 --> 00:01:44.480
So you always have to take what others say
somewhat with a grain of salt while being

00:01:44.480 --> 00:01:50.320
open to if I get a lot of the same opinion
maybe there is something worth looking at

00:01:50.320 --> 00:01:51.320
there.

00:01:51.320 --> 00:01:55.530
I think a lot of it is you have to be like
a detective who is interested in knowing what

00:01:55.530 --> 00:02:00.850
are the things that aren’t working for people
and then I’ll figure out why and try to

00:02:00.850 --> 00:02:01.850
solve them.

00:02:01.850 --> 00:02:05.220
If they say you should do this other thing
instead don’t listen to that.

00:02:05.220 --> 00:02:07.530
Don’t try to do their fixes.

00:02:07.530 --> 00:02:10.952
Listen to what their issues are because they
may not be able to articulate it because people

00:02:10.952 --> 00:02:14.180
try to be helpful and tell you “What if
you try to do this instead?”

00:02:14.180 --> 00:02:17.950
What you really want to know if why did you
not like what I did?

00:02:17.950 --> 00:02:22.350
People sometimes don’t want to say it because
it was too hurtful like “I was too bored.

00:02:22.350 --> 00:02:23.350
I didn’t care.”

00:02:23.350 --> 00:02:24.350
Stuff like that.

00:02:24.350 --> 00:02:27.660
Or they may not even know how to articulate
it they just might know I didn’t like it

00:02:27.660 --> 00:02:33.590
which isn’t helpful which is why we try
to codify some of these things which makes

00:02:33.590 --> 00:02:37.170
someone like or not like something so we can
learn from that.

00:02:37.170 --> 00:02:43.110
So I think it’s that trusting that your
own voice has value and that you will find

00:02:43.110 --> 00:02:48.750
your way to address whatever problems you
decide and agree are problems takes the power

00:02:48.750 --> 00:02:52.800
away from the other random subjective people
who are going to say whatever they are going

00:02:52.800 --> 00:02:53.800
to say.

00:02:53.800 --> 00:02:57.300
Film Courage: I like that, I like ‘becoming
a detective’ and trying to solve it.

00:02:57.300 --> 00:03:01.470
Because in that sense you are taking out some
of the personal feelings from it almost.

00:03:01.470 --> 00:03:07.860
You’re acting as if you’re a third party
and then you’re less upset about it.

00:03:07.860 --> 00:03:09.080
That’s excellent.

00:03:09.080 --> 00:03:13.420
So it’s not something that you’re able
to practice on a daily basis.

00:03:13.420 --> 00:03:19.050
I know 6:00 a.m. hits and it’s a new day
for all of us in the many different things

00:03:19.050 --> 00:03:21.750
that we deal with but it that something that
you really try to implement?

00:03:21.750 --> 00:03:27.050
Erik: Yes, I’m very careful and selective
about when I share stuff and who I share it

00:03:27.050 --> 00:03:32.520
with because I know I’ll have tendency to
get very upset depending on what people say.

00:03:32.520 --> 00:03:35.630
And sometimes it can threaten to derail me
from whatever it is I’m trying to do or

00:03:35.630 --> 00:03:38.110
thinking their is any value in it.

00:03:38.110 --> 00:03:44.310
So I’ve really learned to be careful and
to do it with the right people at the right

00:03:44.310 --> 00:03:45.310
times for the right reasons.

00:03:45.310 --> 00:03:47.180
Film Courage: At the right times, interesting?

00:03:47.180 --> 00:03:51.430
Erik: At the right time of the process because
sometimes you’re ready, it really helps

00:03:51.430 --> 00:03:54.200
if you’ve gone on to another project and
you’re not even thinking about this one

00:03:54.200 --> 00:03:56.480
anymore and to then show it to people.

00:03:56.480 --> 00:03:59.930
Because then if they don’t like it as much
you already have this other one you already

00:03:59.930 --> 00:04:01.340
have this other one that you’re interested
in.

00:04:01.340 --> 00:04:05.410
So I’m a big believe in putting one down,
not even showing it to anyone right away,

00:04:05.410 --> 00:04:09.830
work on something else, come back to the first
one, and you’ll have a new perspective and

00:04:09.830 --> 00:04:12.721
you’ll things you want to change so you
can make your own changes, you never have

00:04:12.721 --> 00:04:14.910
to show it to anyone at that point.

00:04:14.910 --> 00:04:17.880
But eventually when you do show it maybe have
a couple of other things that you’re working

00:04:17.880 --> 00:04:22.360
on so that whole script isn’t your whole
worth anymore.

00:04:22.360 --> 00:04:25.510
Because when you’re working in it, it’s
your baby and you better not have your baby

00:04:25.510 --> 00:04:28.180
get abused by other people right?

00:04:28.180 --> 00:04:31.980
So it helps to get that distance from it.

00:04:31.980 --> 00:04:35.880
And a lot of it is you give yourself distance
from it and are able to give yourself notes

00:04:35.880 --> 00:04:38.100
that you couldn’t do when you were in the
middle of it.

00:04:38.100 --> 00:04:41.620
Film Courage: And you’ve finished it to
some extent whether it was a first draft?

00:04:41.620 --> 00:04:42.620
Erik: To some extent.

00:04:42.620 --> 00:04:45.540
Film Courage: Whereas you’re not “I’m
going to throw this thing away.

00:04:45.540 --> 00:04:46.790
Forget it!

00:04:46.790 --> 00:04:47.790
So and so said this.”

00:04:47.790 --> 00:04:51.010
Because you’ve actually gone through it
so that’s interesting and it’s almost

00:04:51.010 --> 00:04:52.280
like a form of protection.

00:04:52.280 --> 00:04:55.830
Erik: Yes, it’s another thing I got from
The War of Art.

00:04:55.830 --> 00:05:00.110
Steven Pressfield somewhere said he would
finish a project every six months.

00:05:00.110 --> 00:05:03.820
I said to myself ‘Well, I’m going to write
two scripts a year.”

00:05:03.820 --> 00:05:05.970
To the point where I feel they are kind of
finished.

00:05:05.970 --> 00:05:09.910
They’ve gone through multiple drafts of
my own notes, maybe no one else’s notes

00:05:09.910 --> 00:05:15.260
come into play but they’ve really been rewritten
significantly based on my own notes and the

00:05:15.260 --> 00:05:20.620
way I get there is by taking a pause, moving
away, coming back a couple months later to

00:05:20.620 --> 00:05:24.360
finish one but alway have multiple scripts
in the hopper.

00:05:24.360 --> 00:05:28.700
So some are done, some are semi-done, some
we’ve just started so it becomes a little

00:05:28.700 --> 00:05:30.060
bit more of like a factory.

00:05:30.060 --> 00:05:33.580
As opposed to I have this one project that
I’ve been working on for three years and

00:05:33.580 --> 00:05:38.190
it’s all I can think about every day, if
someone doesn’t like it I’m devastated,

00:05:38.190 --> 00:05:40.141
that’s not the most helpful way to go and
I’ve done that.

